    <GaugingStation location="Thornborough Mill" name="Bedford Ouse" number="33005">
        <Comments>
            <Comment type="Station Summary">Flow records generally affected by sluice movement and implementation of digital recording in 1979.</Comment>
            <Comment type="Hydrometric">A bypass channel exists, but operation of gates has ensured the highest flows have been recorded. Significant scatter in gaugings as a result of differing gate positions.</Comment>
            <Comment type="General">Flat V Crump profile weir 10.2m wide and two sluice gates 3.6m broad. Before 1976 weir was broad-crested with centre V notch.</Comment>
            <Comment type="Flow record">Since 1979, peak flow recordings have been digital but recorded only upstream stage, not gate opening and downstream level. Peak flows since 1979 are therefore unreliable and rejected. Daily flows not calculated since Aug 1991. Inaccurate DMF measurement complicated by automatic sluice movement which was not integrated into flow calculation methods. Full period of record peak flow data reviewed and released in September 2023 (WINFAP Files v12).</Comment>
            <Comment type="Flow regime">Flows affected by reservoirs in catchment. Abstraction for public water supply, and industrial and agricultural uses reduces runoff. Runoff increased by effluent returns.</Comment>
            <Comment type="Catchment">The catchment is flat and lies mainly on the Great Oolite. One large tributary drains an area of Oxford Clay. There is a water supply reservoir and a number of ornamental lakes in the catchment.</Comment>
            <Comment type="Factors affecting runoff">SPGEI</Comment>
        </Comments>
